            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Observation: Account Suspensions Hit a Peak</h2>
<p>This week, the technical support team at Yiyu Digital Media received multiple urgent inquiries, all about Google Ads accounts suddenly suspended with all running ads paused. One operations lead for a utility app reported: "Yesterday we were still running campaigns, this morning the account was directly suspended. The backend only showed a vague violation notice with no clear guidance on what to fix."</p>
<p>This is not an isolated case. According to feedback from industry communities this week, account suspensions mainly concentrated on three areas: <strong>non-compliant landing pages</strong>, <strong>ads featuring sensitive elements</strong>, and <strong>misleading conversion paths</strong>. Unlike previous incidents that focused solely on "creative violations," this wave emphasizes the <strong>compliance of the entire投放链路</strong>, meaning fixing creatives alone is no longer sufficient to pass appeals.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three High-Frequency Pitfalls</h2>
<p><strong>1. "Blank" or Malfunctioning Landing Pages</strong></p>
<p>Some teams, rushing to launch quickly, only placed product images and a download button on the landing page, missing necessary information like privacy policies and terms of use. The review team identifies these as "incomplete information pages" and directly triggers suspension.</p>
<p><strong>2. Implied Misleading Statements</strong></p>
<p>Ad copy commonly features absolute terms like "limited-time free" or "lowest price online," or landing pages contain countdown widgets without actual deadlines. Such designs are easily classified as "deceptive behavior."</p>
<p><strong>3. Appeals "Treating Symptoms, Not Causes"</strong></p>
<p>After receiving suspension notices, many operators' first reaction is to submit a text explanation emphasizing "we will be more careful in the future." However, reviewers need <strong>concrete evidence of rectification</strong>, not a promise letter. This distinction directly determines whether the appeal passes.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Appeal Materials Checklist</h2>
<p>The following checklist is compiled based on recent anonymized successful appeal cases, applicable to landing page and creative violations:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Material Type</th>
<th>Specific Requirements</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>URL of the violating page</td>
<td>Provide the specific page link identified as non-compliant</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Screenshots of rectified page</td>
<td>Compare before and after rectification, at least 3 key sections</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Privacy policy link</td>
<td>Confirm a valid, clickable privacy policy is added in the footer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Appeal explanation letter</td>
<td>State the violation reason and rectifications taken</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Verifiable test account</td>
<td>Provide test account or backend screenshots proving normal function</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key Principle</strong>: Each violation reason requires corresponding evidence; do not use one explanation to "package" all issues.</p>
<hr />
<h2>An Anonymized Case</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team promoting home goods had a user review section on their landing page. During review, some reviews mentioning "curing diseases" or "improving sleep" were identified as medical efficacy claims, resulting in a violation warning.</p>
<p>The team's rectification plan:<br />
1. Deleted all efficacy-related review formats<br />
2. Replaced review content with objective descriptions like "satisfied with appearance" and "fast logistics"<br />
3. Added a disclaimer at the top of the page: "This product is a general consumer good and does not have medical efficacy"</p>
<p>The account was reinstated within 48 hours of the appeal. The core lesson: <strong>demonstrate rectification through actions, not words.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>This Week's Recommendations</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Self-inspect the entire chain first after account suspension</strong>—do not rush to submit an appeal. The review team records every appeal submission; repeatedly submitting similar materials will lower your approval rate.</li>
<li><strong>Add landing page compliance review to your workflow starting this week</strong>, especially for pages with user-generated content. We recommend operations teams review common Google ad policy violation points before publishing.</li>
<li><strong>Complete appeal materials within 24 hours</strong> if possible—delays may push your account into secondary review, extending the reinstatement cycle.</li>
</ol>
<p>For further assistance, contact Yiyu Digital Media for account diagnostics and appeal material preparation services.</p>
